nsider the historical trajectory of scientific discovery, with Galileo Galilei serving as a prime example. In Galileo\'s time, the geocentric model was considered "settled science," endorsed by both the scientific community and the Church, where disagreement was a punishable offense. However, Galileo\'s observations through his telescope, particularly of the moons of Jupiter, challenged this long-held belief, supporting instead the heliocentric model proposed by Copernicus. This case illustrates how what is deemed "settled" can be upended by new evidence or innovative perspectives. \nScience thrives on skepticism and the willingness to revise theories in light of new data. Labeling science as settled stifles this process, discouraging the kind of revolutionary thinking Galileo embodied, which is essential for scientific progress.', 'entity_set': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': []}, 'richtext': {'richtext_tags': []}, 'media': {'inline_media': []}}}}, 'legacy': {'bookmark_count': 1, 'bookmarked': False, 'created_at': 'Thu Jan 30 23:59:25 +0000 2025', 'conversation_id_str': '1885020473618055460', 'display_text_range': [14, 292], 'entities': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'timestamps': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': [{'id_str': '69156861', 'name': 'Chief Nerd', 'screen_name': 'TheChiefNerd', 'indices': [0, 13]}]}, 'favorite_count': 3, 'favorited': False, 'full_text': '@TheChiefNerd The concept of "settled science" is fundamentally flawed, reflecting a misunderstanding of the scientific process. Consider the historical trajectory of scientific discovery, with Galileo Galilei serving as a prime example. In Galileo\'s time, the geocentric model was considered', 'in_reply_to_screen_name': 'TheChiefNerd', 'in_reply_to_status_id_str': '1885020473618055460', 'in_reply_to_user_id_str': '69156861', 'is_quote_status': False, 'lang': 'en', 'quote_count': 0, 'reply_count': 0, 'retweet_count': 1, 'retweeted': False, 'user_id_str': '4454808919', 'id_st